  "live_records": {
    "q-117348": {
      "dom_id": "step-q117348",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base and a lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $2\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ What is the radius of the cylinder that gives the smallest surface area?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/33c90b487d2b3036068acf4b771d2ca6ae95e8e8446b706b85fed058dc12a0bf.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $2\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =4\\,000\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =1\\,000 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} \\\\ r & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=10$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=10\\,\\text{cm}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
