    "q-18447": {
      "dom_id": "step-q18447",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $64\\pi \\,{mm}^{3}.$ What is the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/cec96bdd2335ff5635d11c4ea6016257b35bd5450778a70ecc1f5e0156ccfe9e.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $64\\pi \\,{mm}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n64\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{64}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{64}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =128\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =64 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ r & =4\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nSo the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=4\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{mm}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =\\frac{128\\pi }{4}+\\pi (4)^{2} \\\\  & =32\\pi +16\\pi  \\\\  & =48\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=48\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
