    "q-18451": {
      "dom_id": "step-q18451",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base and a lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $686\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}.$ What is the smallest surface we can get?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/cec96bdd2335ff5635d11c4ea6016257b35bd5450778a70ecc1f5e0156ccfe9e.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $686\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n686\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{686}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{686}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =1\\,372\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =343 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{343} \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<7$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>7$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=7$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=7\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=7\\,\\text{mm}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(7) & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{7}+2\\pi (7)^{2} \\\\  & =294\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=294\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.$",
