    "q-18444": {
      "dom_id": "step-q18444",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/50853fcaa44ff9b7d80e708e546275286541afc0b6ed05c4ca87ea3175364c09.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $48\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the largest possible volume of the cylinder?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $48\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=48\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =48\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{48\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{48-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{48-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(48r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=\\frac{(48-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(48-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 48 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ r & =4\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=4$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(4)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nSo the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the largest volume, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{cm}$ into the equation for the volume $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(4) & =\\frac{(48(4)-(4)^{3})\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(192-64)\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =64\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the largest possible volume of the cylinder is $V=64\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
