      "source_example_id": "e-352",
      "knowledge_point": "Maximizing the Volume of a Cylinder With a Fixed Surface Area",
      "kp_id": "e-352"
    },
    "q-117360": {
      "dom_id": "step-q117360",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/08f6351b8b95c87386d07466b6e876d72f33bc00034e83b360d03d23633d363d.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with a lid using $6\\pi \\,{ft}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $6\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}(\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}) \\\\ V(r) & =(3r-{r}^{3})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(3-3{r}^{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (3-3{r}^{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 3 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =1 \\\\ r & =1\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=1$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=-6\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(1)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=1$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=1\\,\\text{ft}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
