    "q-345215": {
      "dom_id": "step-q345215",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $\\frac{125}{3}\\pi \\,{yd}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$7.50$ per ${yd}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$2.50$ per ${yd}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius $r$ of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{yd}$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/55de2c06ea57724513e450cbf49a0477d63cd75bac601d5d95f0ba7e366852ff.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 7.50+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 2.50 \\\\  & =15\\pi rh+2.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $\\frac{125}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{yd}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{125}{3}\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{125}{3{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =15\\pi r(\\frac{125}{3{r}^{2}})+2.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{625\\pi }{r}+2.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+5\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+5\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 5\\pi r & =\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 5\\pi {r}^{3} & =625\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =125 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=5$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=5\\,\\text{yd}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
