    "q-86220": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86220",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $72\\pi \\,{ft}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$1.5$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$0.5$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/959050ee306a96db3745f84bc7095d4e83a23ceadd819f0b749c04b20d5778d2.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 1.5+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3\\pi rh+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $72\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n72\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =3\\pi r(\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}})+0.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{216\\pi }{r}+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r & =0 \\\\ \\pi r & =\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ \\pi {r}^{3} & =216\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =216 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{216} \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=6\\,\\text{ft}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
