    "q-315673": {
      "dom_id": "step-q315673",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $9\\pi \\,{ft}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$4.50$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$1.50$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius $r$ of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/6393c2cbff5d727841ca78ac067ff7067114d6db8754454a30584f38e4a4ea87.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 4.50+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 1.50 \\\\  & =9\\pi rh+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $9\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n9\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =9\\pi r(\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}})+1.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{r}+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 3\\pi r & =\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 3\\pi {r}^{3} & =81\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=3\\,\\text{ft}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
